  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\n**** Mid-Week Rainfall *****\n\nTwo short waves are still expected to move into the region and\nthen phase together over central Illinois and bring rain and\nisolated storms to the eastern part of the CWA and east of the\nstate tomorrow and tomorrow night. The associated low pressure\narea that develops will track right across central Illinois\ntomorrow, but any precip with this system will be minimal and\nmodels continue to decrease the amount of QPF and keep most of\nthe precip over eastern parts of the CWA. This is all due to the\ntiming of the phasing of the two troughs over the area and that\nwhen the system depends/merges, the system will mostly be east\nof the state. In fact, HiRes models show no precip over western\nportions of the CWA. SBCAPEs are forecasted to be between\n500-1000 j/kg ahead of the low tomorrow afternoon, but then the\nstrongest dynamics with the system will be mainly east of I-57\nby then. SPC Day 2 outlook as made adjustments for this with the\ngreatest threat of strong to damaging winds being mainly in IN.\nHowever, will still include an isolated thunderstorm threat in\nthe east in the afternoon for when the system begins it merging.\n\n**** End of Week Cool-Down ****\n\nAs the system pushes east the 500mb trough moves in behind into\nThursday and given the cold air advection expected behind the\nfront and the dynamics associated with the trough, believe\nisolated to scattered showers will be possible Thursday \nafternoon after a bit of increase to the lapse rates. Higher\npops will be possible in the east and northeast parts of the CWA\nas the focus shifts to lake-enhanced showers from Lake Michigan\npossibly clipping parts of Champaign, Vermilion, and Edgar\ncounties. The other focus for Thursday will be the windy and\nmuch cooler conditions arriving into the area. High temperatures\nwill only reach the middle to upper 40s across much of the \nregion...with lower 50s south of I-70. Northwest winds gusting\nto 20-25mph will make it feel even cooler as wind-chill values \nhover in the 30s for much of the day. The coolest period will \nbe Friday and Friday night, with highs and lows well below \nnormal for this time of year. Highs will drop into the lower to\nmiddle 40s during the day, with lows in the lower to middle 20s.\nAfter that, the trough will shift to the east and northeast by\nearly next week. As a result, temperatures will rise back to\nmore seasonal levels in the mid to upper 50s for next Monday \nand Tuesday.",
